Thanks!
I've committed the patch implementing remove() directly, but opened a pull request for the change to the Exception thrown ScriptEngine.eval()

I'm a little nervous about this change as I don't know whether there are people out there specifically catching EvalException. Reading the Javadoc for ScriptEngine, it does indeed seem that we would be expected to throw a ScriptException "if an error occurs in script."

Anyone out there have any feelings on this?

Might hold off until we bump the major version in a month or so.

Thanks again,
Alex

On Mon, Jul 4, 2016 at 10:25 AM Horváth Péter Gergely <peter.gerg...@gmail.com> wrote:
> Hi Alex,
>
> I have two small changes for RenjinBindings and RenjinScriptEngine, which you could probably look into. I hope you think they are OK so they can make their way to the code base. :)
>
> 1.) I've implemented remove(String) operation on RenjinBindings; the following small piece of code seems to work for me:
>
> @Override
> public Object remove(Object key) {
>   Object originalValue = get(key);
>   frame.remove(toSymbol(key));
>   return originalValue;
> }
>
> 2.) I think it would make sense if Renjin emitted the standard ScriptException type in case of any evaluation failure instead of the internal EvalException in RenjinScriptEngine, so I would propose the following minor adjustments in org.renjin.script.RenjinScriptEngine#eval(org.renjin.eval.Context, org.renjin.sexp.SEXP):
>
> private Object eval(Context context, SEXP source) throws ScriptException {
>   try {
>     return context.evaluate( source, context.getEnvironment());
>   } catch(BreakException e) {
>     throw new ScriptException("no loop for break");
>   } catch(NextException e) {
>     throw new ScriptException("no loop for next");
>   } catch (EvalException e) {
>     throw new ScriptException(e);
>   }
>
> }
>
> Please find attached both changes as patches. Note: I haven't run a full Renjin-build with my changes, I just monkey-patched the classes in my environment.
>
> I am not sure how to implement javax.script.ScriptContext.setAttribute(String, Object, int) for Renjin, this is something you probably want to consider looking into a bit later. For us, having one approach that works is fine.
>
> Thanks,
> Peter
